[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) said the dates bug was one issue with one fix queued. The reports
said otherwise. Their cause depends on the tenant (where they are, and which booking form they
run), and `causeOf()` in `src/lib/kb/causes.ts` finds more than one.

<Callout kind="warn">
Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October. Parser: fixed (<code>fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales</code>). Hint: won't fix, because the booking-v3 form was retired.
</Callout>

## What the table shows

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The `tagged as` column is what intake guessed from the reporter's browser. It disagrees with
the cause on many reports, so do not triage on it.

## The problems

1. **Parser.** The shared parser reads every date month first, and these tenants write the day
   first. Most of #4471 is this, including a few reports the desk read as a move of one day (their own cause is not confirmed). Ticket: [#1](https://github.com/discoduckling/kb-4471-what-it-shows/issues/1).
2. **Hint.** On booking-v3, the form tells day-first tenants to type `MM/DD/YYYY`, and they do.
   A parser fix does not change this. Ticket: [#2](https://github.com/discoduckling/kb-4471-what-it-shows/issues/2).

## Why it was merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone, and nobody reads
the body. The subjects in this queue were all variants of "wrong day", so they matched. The
rule works for one bug reported many times. It fails when one subject covers several bugs.
A new decision record should replace 0001; this page does not edit it.

## Lessons

- Count by cause before you merge. A big ticket can be several bugs under one subject.
- Do not trust the intake tag. It is a guess made before anyone looks up the tenant.

